<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    A class library, never the web SDK: this layer persists and secures, it does
    not host HTTP. So no ASP.NET Core package and no framework reference to the
    ASP.NET Core shared framework may be added here.

    The reference on Application is deliberate rather than incidental: some of
    the abstractions this layer implements are declared there, and its options
    classes are bound here through IOptions<T>. Never reference DnnMigration.Api,
    which already references this project and would invert the graph, and never
    reference a test project or a legacy project under Library/ or Website/.

    This project owns every Entity Framework Core, SQL client, password-hashing,
    health-check and token-signing reference in the solution, and nothing else.
    Swagger, versioning and logging belong to Api; validation belongs to
    Application; the test database providers belong to the integration test
    project, because shipping one here would put a test provider into production.

    JWT is deliberately split across two projects rather than owned by one,
    because minting and validating a token are different jobs living in different
    layers. This project mints — JwtTokenService signs the access token — so it
    references the signing library. Api validates every inbound token on the
    request path, which is middleware, so it references the ASP.NET Core bearer
    authentication handler. Moving either reference to the other project would
    force one of those two responsibilities into a layer that must not hold it.

    Build policy is inherited from backend/Directory.Build.props, which is why
    this file declares no PropertyGroup. Restating or overriding an inherited
    property would weaken the Release build's warnings-as-errors policy.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>

    <!--
      The 8.0.29 band matches the installed shared framework exactly. Every
      version here is exact-pinned: no floating range, wildcard or moving tag.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" Version="8.0.29" />

    <!--
      Provider for the existing, unaltered SQL Server database. The legacy
      provider registration uses an empty object qualifier and "dbo" as the
      database owner, so those are the defaults entity configurations bind to.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer" Version="8.0.29" />

    <!--
      Keep this reference PLAIN — adding PrivateAssets="all", the shape the stock
      template uses, stops the design-time library flowing to referencing
      projects. The EF CLI resolves design-time services from the startup
      project, and the API project receives this package only transitively from
      here, so severing the flow breaks migration tooling with the familiar
      "startup project does not reference Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design"
      error. The separate command-line tooling package is not referenced;
      dotnet-ef is installed as a global tool.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="8.0.29" />

    <!--
      Explicit anti-downgrade pin: without this line the SQL Server provider
      resolves Microsoft.Data.SqlClient 5.1.7 transitively. It is not redundant —
      list the package graph with transitive dependencies included and it must
      report 5.2.3.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.SqlClient" Version="5.2.3" />

    <!--
      One-way password hashing, replacing a reversibly-encrypted credential store
      whose key was committed to the legacy configuration. Existing credentials
      cannot be verified against a one-way hash and this solution contains no
      legacy verifier, so the migration path is an ADMINISTRATIVE RESET ONLY.
      Do not describe it as a re-hash on first successful login: no code path can
      perform that, because verifying the legacy credential is the very step that
      is missing.
      Security/BcryptPasswordHasher.cs uses this package's ENHANCED hash and
      verify pair, which pre-digests the credential and so removes the plain
      algorithm's 72-byte input-equivalence behaviour; the two halves must always
      be changed together. The legacy password policy is preserved rather than
      tightened mid-migration. Both divergences are recorded in
      MIGRATION_NOTES.md.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="BCrypt.Net-Next" Version="4.0.3" />

    <!--
      Database probe behind /health. That endpoint must stay anonymous and
      reachable: docker-compose gates the frontend service on the API becoming
      healthy, and the Alpine runtime image probes it with wget rather than curl.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="AspNetCore.HealthChecks.SqlServer" Version="8.0.2" />

    <!--
      Access-token signing for JwtTokenService. This is the token-handling library
      only, not the ASP.NET Core authentication middleware: nothing here touches
      the request pipeline, which is why a class library may reference it.

      The 7.7.3 pin is not arbitrary and must not be raised independently. The
      Api project's Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer 8.0.29 resolves
      Microsoft.IdentityModel.Protocols.OpenIdConnect 7.7.3, which pulls
      Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens 7.7.3 — the very assembly this package also
      depends on. Pinning the same 7.7.3 band means the token this project signs
      and the token Api validates are handled by one loaded assembly version
      rather than two, so a signature that verifies in a unit test also verifies
      on the request path. Bump both together or neither.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt" Version="7.7.3" />

  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DnnMigration.Domain\DnnMigration.Domain.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DnnMigration.Application\DnnMigration.Application.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ONE friend assembly, and one only: the unit test project.

    Why it is needed. Security/BcryptPasswordHasher.cs and Security/PermissionEvaluator.cs
    are the two places in this solution where a credential is turned into a stored digest
    and where a grant is turned into an access decision. AAP 0.5.1.5 names
    Infrastructure/Security/*.cs as the source of
    tests/DnnMigration.UnitTests/Security/{BcryptPasswordHasher,JwtTokenService,
    PermissionEvaluator}Tests.cs, with "Hash round-trip, token claims, permission
    resolution" as what those suites are for, and AAP 0.2.1.1 places the
    permission-evaluator tests in that same project. Both types are internal sealed, so
    without this item the suites can only assert the shape of the abstractions and a
    stand-in of their documented behaviour - which is exactly the false-green coverage a
    review found: the real enhanced-BCrypt pair, the policy and byte-ceiling guards, the
    malformed-digest handlers, the work-factor replacement signal and the whole
    allow-and-deny reduction could all regress with a green suite. Proving them needs the
    concrete types, and the concrete types are here.

    What it does NOT weaken, which is the point. The production dependency graph is
    untouched: Domain still declares zero project references and zero package references,
    DnnMigration.Application still cannot see this assembly, and an accidental
    infrastructure dependency in Application or Domain is still a compile error rather
    than a review comment. That guarantee comes from what the PRODUCTION project files
    reference, and a test project is not one of them - it sits outside the layered graph
    as its outermost consumer. AAP Rules T1 and T3 order the four production layers; they
    say nothing about a test assembly, and reading them as if they did is what left these
    two components unproven.

    Bounds. Add no second friend assembly. In particular DnnMigration.IntegrationTests is
    deliberately absent: it reaches this layer legitimately through Api and the container,
    resolving the public registrations, and naming an internal type from there would let
    an integration test bypass the composition it exists to exercise. And no production
    project may be added, ever - that is not what this mechanism is for.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="DnnMigration.UnitTests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
